description:
Run the emem-guard policy checks on text before you send it. The checks use this responder's corpus. The tool finds every emem: citation, resolves each one, and returns allow or deny with a reason that a machine can read: `EMEM-GUARD DENY <CODE> token=<token|-> fix=<fix> leaf=<leaf|->`. The codes are: PROV_SIG (the signature did not verify). PROV_BYTES (the citation resolved to different content). PROV_DRIFT (the reading moved past its band threshold). PROV_VALUE (a stated value does not agree with the cited fact). CLAIM_UNGROUNDED (a measurable claim has no citation; turn it on with claim_gating). `fix` tells you what to do: refresh_token, remove_reference, contact_admin, cite_observation, correct_value. Only a self-hosted guard returns GEO_ZONE and redact_and_retry. This tool is ADVISORY: it blocks nothing. A citation that this responder does not hold is never a denial, because it can be a citation from another responder. Memory algebra: the `verify` operation (https://emem.dev/docs/model.html).

When to use: Call it on your draft before you assert something, or on a tool result before you reason on it. Then you can fix a citation that does not resolve. With `claim_gating: true`, it also names measurable claims with no citation, and the band that answers each. For a payload from another framework (CloudEvent, OPA input, OpenAI moderations body, a tool call from another server), send it unchanged and name its `shape`. The default reader reads only `texts`, and a check that read nothing answers allow. To ENFORCE the policy, use emem_guard_selfhost. It returns the procedure for your own node.

Example arguments: {"texts":["Lusail ground sits at 2.6 m per emem:fact:defi.zb521.jawI.gOze:f3yr3urkrfwamlemwadulgth2qpw645vi6l6fhtuu4dy5o7onahq"]}
